Z||000016||batboy||12-06-1999||12:28 AM||||That's another eBay scam. They put an outragiously high reserve price on it and if the reserve is not met, then the seller spams all the bidders via email and tells them that the highest bid was X amount and the reserve was Y amount, but he'll "sacrifice" it to the first person who offers Z amount. If the reserve is met, then the seller laughs all the way to the bank. Sometimes people go crazy at auctions, that's why it's a popular way to sell stuff. I've seen crappy things at estate auctions sell for more than what they cost new at the store (not talking antiques). People get caught up in the excitement of bidding and want to "win".||||reg||1
Z||000017||jlhuber||12-06-1999||12:41 AM||||you know, i honestly never thought that a gm motor and drivetrain would fit in a hand built exotic italian sports car.  or that anyone would want to replace the original ferrari motor with a gm thus reducing the value of the car by over 500%.  i'm sorry, but a gm motor sounds nothing like a ferrari motor.  ferrari's get their hp from the high rpms that they run, around 10grand and up, so you can imagine what they sound like.  i wonder if that guy would sell my the 308's original motor, since he obviously has no use for it.   no doubt, it is a kit car.||||reg||13
